WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.841
A double integral accumulates tiny contributions over a region in two input dimensions

00:00:05.841 --> 00:00:15.467
For the surface z equals x plus y above the unit square, each small base rectangle contributes its area times the surface height.

00:00:16.767 --> 00:00:19.294
Integrate one coordinate at a time

00:00:19.294 --> 00:00:23.929
During the inner integration with respect to x, y stays constant

00:00:23.929 --> 00:00:31.933
The result is one half plus y, representing the cross-sectional accumulation for each fixed value of the outer coordinate.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:38.173
Now add those cross-sectional accumulations over y from zero to one

00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:43.113
One half y plus one half y squared increases by one

00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:49.400
Continuity on this rectangle justifies the iterated integration and also permits reversing the order.

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:51.952
Bounds encode geometry

00:00:51.952 --> 00:00:59.467
Over the triangle below y equals x in the unit square, the upper y bound depends on x

00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:07.400
Integrating one over that triangle returns its area, one half; using constant square bounds would describe a different region.
